 ## Overview
-Lookup Table Addressing for Resampling is a method to compute memory offsets for accessing neighboring samples in a brick-structured volume without using conditional branches or expensive division/modulo operations [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently addressing the 7-neighborhood (for resampling) or 26-neighborhood (for gradient computation) required by volume raycasting, where samples may span across brick boundaries [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike the parent method [Brick Volume Addressing with If-Else](3340-brick-volume-addressing-with-if-else.md), which uses `if-else` statements to check boundary conditions, this method uses a precomputed global lookup table indexed by a bitwise-derived case identifier, eliminating branches and ensuring cache-friendly access [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Lookup Table Addressing for Resampling computes memory offsets for accessing neighboring samples in a brick-structured volume without conditional branches or division/modulo operations [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently addressing the 7-neighborhood (resampling) or 26-neighborhood (gradient computation) required by volume raycasting, where samples may span across brick boundaries [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike the parent method [Brick Volume Addressing with If-Else](3340-brick-volume-addressing-with-if-else.md), which uses `if-else` statements to check boundary conditions, this method uses a precomputed global lookup table indexed by a bitwise-derived case identifier, eliminating branches and ensuring cache-friendly access [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
