 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Power of Two Constraint:** The index calculation relies on brick dimensions being powers of two [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Power of Two Constraint:** The index calculation relies on brick dimensions being powers of two [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 - **Power of Two Apart:** The resampling index formula assumes dimensions are "a power of two apart" to ensure the sum of masked values maps uniquely to $[0,7]$ via the shift [S1, unofficial]. If dimensions are not distinct powers of two, the shift amount $\min(N_x, N_y, N_z)$ may cause collisions [S1, unofficial]. The source suggests introducing a simple shift operation to virtually maintain the constraint or removing the constraint by other means [S1, unofficial].
-- **Boundary Conditions:** The method handles brick boundaries implicitly by mapping boundary samples to specific cases in the LUT [S1, S2, unofficial]. No explicit boundary checks are needed in the kernel [S1, S2, unofficial].
